The used battery electric vehicle (BEV) market is growing, yet consumers face substantial uncertainty about battery condition at the point of sale. This paper estimates consumer preferences and willingness to pay (WTP) for vehicle attributes in the used BEV market using a discrete choice experiment (DCE) administered to a national sample of 3,072 U.S. adults. The DCE jointly varies conventional vehicle attributes (price, mileage) and battery-specific attributes (electric range, degradation rate, refurbishment history) across six repeated choice tasks. A mixed logit model found that consumers value lower mileage and additional range, and penalize range degradation and battery refurbishment. The model also confirms substantial preference heterogeneity at the population level. The latent class choice model identifies six consumer segments that differ markedly in their sensitivity to battery quality signals, and the no-choice option, which can be further explained by their distinctive characteristics. An information treatment analysis further shows that providing detailed battery replacement cost and warranty information does not affect overall market (dis)engagement, but reshapes the type of concern driving opt-out behavior. These findings carry direct implications for used vehicle dealers, OEMs, and certification service providers seeking to develop market infrastructure for used BEVs.

